@subsection Why single keys work here at all

These viewports sit in Evil @emph{emacs state}, which is set per major
mode.  In normal or motion state @kbd{h}, @kbd{j}, @kbd{k}, @kbd{l},
@kbd{n}, @kbd{a}, @kbd{e} and @kbd{c} are Evil motions and operators, so
every one of them would do something else --- famously @kbd{j} going to
@code{evil-next-line} and reporting ``end of buffer''.  @key{escape}
leaves for normal state, @kbd{C-z} comes back, and @kbd{C-w} keeps
Evil's window commands throughout.

Each mode has to declare this for itself: @code{evil-set-initial-state}
is looked up per mode, and inheriting from a parent that declares it is
not enough.  Until it did, the second brain was a mouse-only tool under
Doom --- every key silently did something else.

@subsection Navigating

Four tiers, mirroring @code{cmacs-roamgraph} so the siblings feel like
one tool.

@table @asis
@item Spatial (@kbd{h j k l}, arrows)
The nearest node inside a screen-space cone in that direction.  Moves
are @emph{exactly reversible}: a cone metric is not self-inverse, so
going back the way you came is replayed from a trail rather than
recomputed, and @kbd{l} then @kbd{h} returns you rather than landing
somewhere new.  The cone is wider than roamgraph's
(@code{cmacs-secondbrain-cone-degrees}, 50 degrees) because a ring
layout puts your neighbour at an angle from you rather than beside you.

@item Topological (@kbd{]} @kbd{[} @kbd{>} @kbd{<} @kbd{o})
Walk links, and walk the department you are standing in.  Links are
treated as undirected --- the hub-to-member edges point one way and the
org-roam links the other, and someone tracing connections does not care
which end the data happened to store first.  @kbd{]} will not
immediately walk back the way you arrived, so it cannot degenerate into
a two-node oscillation.

@item Hierarchical (@kbd{^} @kbd{m})
The ARMS tier, which roamgraph has no equivalent of: a node belongs to a
department, which belongs to a ring.

@item Camera (@kbd{f} @kbd{+} @kbd{-} @kbd{0})
@kbd{f} flies to the selection @emph{and re-aims the camera's target at
it}, so zooming and orbiting afterwards revolve around that node rather
than around wherever the view happened to be centred.  @kbd{+} and
@kbd{-} then close and open that distance; each notch takes a fixed
fraction of what is left, so steps shrink as you approach and you
cannot overshoot.

@item Search (@kbd{/} @kbd{n} @kbd{N} @kbd{J} @kbd{o})
See @ref{Secondbrain Search}.
@end table

@subsection The camera follows only when it must

A spatial step deliberately does @emph{not} move the camera: the node
you stepped to is normally right there, and moving the view on every
keystroke makes the whole map swim under you.

But the ring layout is much larger than the viewport, so a few steps in
one direction walk the selection off the edge --- and from then on you
are navigating something you cannot see.  So the camera follows on
exactly the condition that matters: when the new selection is not on
screen, inset by @code{cmacs-secondbrain-edge-margin} (48 px) so a node
half-clipped by the edge counts as off-screen rather than visible.
@code{cmacs-secondbrain-keep-selection-visible} turns it off.

@subsection Navigation reveals

The map opens collapsed, so most of what you can search for is not on
screen --- and selecting a node with no drawable sets no scene
selection, draws no halo and moves no camera.  That is
indistinguishable from the key having done nothing.

So every keyboard jump goes through one choke point that expands the
ancestors it needs first, outermost first, then flies the camera.  It is
a single function precisely so that no individual command can forget.

@subsection Mouse

@multitable @columnfractions .38 .62
@headitem Gesture @tab Effect
@item left click on a department
  @tab select it @strong{and expand or fold it}
@item left click on a leaf @tab select it; the inspector follows
@item double click a node @tab select it and fly the camera to it
@item left drag a node @tab move it, and pin it where you drop it
@item a short drag @tab is still a click; dragging starts past a few pixels
@item left drag @tab orbit (3D); no effect in the locked 2D view
@item right drag @tab pan
@item wheel @tab zoom, wheel-up towards the scene
@item hover anything @tab light up its whole department, dim the rest
@item right click @tab the context menu for that kind, AI section last
@end multitable

Clicking a department expands it because that is the whole interaction:
the rings tell you what exists, and a click tells you what is inside.
Making expand a separate keystroke would mean the obvious gesture on the
obvious target does nothing.

@strong{A click does not move the camera.}  libregnum's input layer
flies to whatever a left click hits --- right for a scene you navigate
@emph{by} clicking, wrong here, where the click starts an animation and
the camera arriving on top of the hub hides the one thing you clicked to
see.  Flying is @kbd{f}, deliberately, and it keeps
@code{cmacs-secondbrain-fly-context} (0.4 by default) of the whole
graph's extent between camera and node: the node's own size says nothing
about the scale of the graph around it, so without that floor a fly-to
frames one sphere and none of its surroundings.

@subsection Background

@kbd{b} picks what fills the viewport behind the graph: @code{nebula}
(the default --- layered value noise plus a star field over a vertical
gradient), @code{starfield}, @code{gradient}, @code{solid}, @code{none},
or @code{image}, which draws a wallpaper file cover-fit so any aspect
ratio fills the viewport without being squashed.

Picking @code{image} offers whatever it finds under
@code{cmacs-secondbrain-wallpaper-dirs} and still accepts any path you
type.  An unreadable path is refused and reported rather than blanking
the viewport.

The procedural kinds are generated once into a texture and cached until
the kind, colours or viewport size change, so they cost one blit per
frame.  They are deterministic --- the same viewport gives the same sky
every session, which is both less distracting and what makes them
testable.

@strong{Screensavers} are a background too, though not the default: set
@code{cmacs-secondbrain-background} to @code{screensaver} and pick a
config with @code{cmacs-secondbrain-screensaver} (@code{helios-blue} if
you do not).  They render in the same out-of-process
renderer the animated wallpaper uses, so none of the screensaver's GL
runs on Emacs's thread, and the sink they use needs no gowl --- this
works in a plain pgtk Emacs.

Pick a dark one.  The graph is drawn over the top, and a screensaver
that fills the frame with bright detail leaves the nodes nowhere to be
read against.

If the screensaver subsystem is not built, or the module will not load,
the background falls back to @code{nebula} and says so rather than
leaving a blank viewport.

@subsection Labels

Label size is not a fixed pixel count.  It is a base
(@code{cmacs-secondbrain-label-size}, 15) that applies at a viewport
@code{cmacs-secondbrain-label-reference-height} tall and scales with the
actual viewport from there, capped by
@code{cmacs-secondbrain-label-scale-max}.

That indirection earns its keep because the framebuffer now tracks the
window exactly: one number cannot read well in both a half-screen window
and a maximised one on a large display, and a value chosen for the
smaller is unreadably small in the larger.  It is re-applied on every
refit, which is precisely when the viewport changed.

The font is baked at 48 px and drawn smaller --- downscaling through the
bilinear filter is what keeps the glyphs sharp, and a label drawn
@emph{above} its baked size would be upscaled, which is the blur this
avoids.  The plate behind each label is opaque: it exists for the case
where the scene behind the text is a ring of hundreds of nodes with a
thousand links over it, and any translucency there puts that texture
straight through the glyphs.

@subsection Connections

Selecting a note @strong{lights its links and dims everything else}.
That is the part that makes them legible: at rest every link is drawn
the same, which is honest and useless once there are a few hundred ---
the answer to ``what is this note connected to'' is invisible inside its
own hairball.

The light travels as a @strong{rope light}: each lit link carries a run
of evenly spaced bright beads that all advance together, so the
direction is read off the run rather than off any single bead.  The link
itself is the @emph{tube} --- drawn in the node's own colour at moderate
brightness --- and the beads are nearly white, because lamps only
slightly brighter than their tube are not lamps.

That colouring is the other half of making links legible: a lit link
says @emph{whose} link it is, where a grey line says nothing.

It is a fixed pool of beads repositioned every frame, not one drawable
per link --- a note with a hundred links would otherwise add a hundred
drawables on every selection change, and removing them again means a
rebuild.  The pool is shared out over the lit links, so many links get
fewer beads each rather than most links getting none.

@code{cmacs-secondbrain-link-pulse} turns it off;
@code{-link-pulse-speed} sets how fast.  Note the pattern repeats every
bead-span: identical, evenly spaced beads advancing by exactly one span
give a pixel-identical frame.

@strong{The linked notes show their names.}  A lit rope running off to
an unlabelled dot says something is connected without saying what.
Selecting a note flags everything it links to, and a flagged node gets a
label priority tier of its own --- eligibility alone is not enough,
because labels are capped and the cap ranks by node size, so a small
linked note loses to every hub in the scene.

@strong{Selecting a department lights what the department connects to.}
A hub carries no org-roam links of its own --- only its members do --- so
matching the selected node alone lit nothing at all.  Membership walks
the parent chain, so a note nested below a folder below a hub still
counts.

Selecting from Lisp --- keyboard navigation, search, the inspector ---
tells the scene too, so those get the halo and the lit links exactly as
a click does.

@subsection Dragging and rotation

Left-drag a node to move it.  It is @strong{pinned} where you drop it,
so the layout leaves it alone; @kbd{u} unpins the selection and @kbd{U}
unpins everything.  Empty space still orbits and pans, so the camera
controls are unchanged.

@kbd{R} turns on a slow rotation.  It is the layout's own spin, so the
nodes really move and picking follows them --- not a camera trick, which
would look the same and leave every pick box behind.  It costs a layout
pass per tick, which is why it is off unless you ask.

@subsection Node shading

raylib draws an unlit sphere in a single colour, and the renderer's real
lighting reaches only @code{MESH_ASSET} models inside the editor build
--- so a graph of a few thousand spheres has no shading available to it
at all, and every node is a flat disc with size as its only depth cue.

Sphere-shaped nodes are therefore drawn as @strong{impostors}: a
camera-facing quad carrying a texture of a lit sphere (ambient, diffuse,
a tight specular, and a rim term, over a soft circular alpha mask).  One
texture serves every node, tinted per node.

@strong{Why an impostor and not a highlight.}  The obvious cheap trick
is a small bright sphere offset toward the light, and it does not
survive contact with the 3D view: a world-space offset is only correct
for one camera position.  Orbit and the highlight slides round the back
of the node and vanishes.  A camera-facing quad is correct from every
angle by construction, and costs nothing per frame to keep that way.

Three details that are load-bearing rather than cosmetic:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{The tint is the ceiling.}  The texture is multiplied by the
tint, so a node tinted with its own colour could never be brighter than
that colour and the specular would have nowhere to go.  The tint is
lifted toward white --- but only slightly, because the colour @emph{is}
the PARA category here.  Lift it far and every ring drifts toward the
same pale wash.
@item
@strong{Mipmaps.}  Nodes are drawn a few pixels across, and minifying a
256px texture that far without mipmaps shimmers on every camera move.
The artefact is motion, so a still screenshot hides it completely.
@item
@strong{Billboards are not cleared by @code{clear-drawables}.}  The
scene clears them itself on rebuild, or every refresh appends another
impostor per node --- thousands of stale quads at the positions the
nodes used to be in, which a screenshot of a freshly built scene hides
because the newest ones are drawn in the right places.
@end itemize

Skills and applications keep their real geometry: a gem must show facets
and a prism must show edges, and an impostor of a sphere shows neither.
Those get the small offset highlight instead, which is adequate because
there are tens of them rather than thousands.

@code{cmacs-secondbrain-node-shading} turns the whole thing off.

@subsection Glow

Every node also carries a soft additive halo in its own colour, drawn by
a dedicated renderer pass after everything else: additive blending,
depth writes off, depth testing on.  Each of those three is
load-bearing.  @emph{Additive} means a halo can only brighten what is
under it, overlapping halos sum instead of occluding, and draw order
stops mattering --- which is why the pass needs no sorting.
@emph{Depth writes off} is the particle lesson: a translucent quad that
writes depth punches an invisible hole nothing behind it can draw
through.  @emph{Depth testing on} is what anchors a halo to its node.

The halo carries state in intensity rather than in colour --- hubs and
the centre glow wider and brighter, search matches glow gold, dimmed
nodes barely at all, and the @emph{selection breathes}: its halo swells
and settles on the same clock as the travelling link light.

The same change fixed a latent artefact in the billboard pass itself:
alpha-blended camera-facing quads were drawn in insertion order, and a
quad's translucent texels still write depth --- so once the camera
orbited and two nodes overlapped, the nearer quad's soft edge clipped
the farther one into a rectangle.  The normal billboard layer is now
depth-sorted back-to-front every frame.

@code{cmacs-secondbrain-node-glow} (default @code{t}) and the @kbd{G}
key control it.  Like shading, it is decided when the scene is built, so
the toggle re-reads the sources.

@subsection Particles

Ambient particles drift from every department hub, coloured by its PARA
bucket and breathing wider and faster the more it holds, so the motion
carries the same information the size does.  Selecting a node and
expanding a department each fire a burst --- the expand's is bigger and
bluer, because it moves the whole department and the burst marks where
from.

They are decoration and nothing about the map's meaning depends on them.
They are on because a system you are meant to feel ownership of should
look alive; @kbd{P} turns them off, and @code{cmacs-secondbrain-particles}
is the defcustom.

@strong{The animation clock stops when nobody is looking.}  A tick
renders the whole scene synchronously, and Emacs runs timers whenever
it @emph{waits} --- including inside @code{accept-process-output}
during startup.  A clock left running for a buffer no window is
showing therefore renders on top of whatever Emacs is trying to do,
indefinitely.  Since the link pulse has no end state, a selected node
used to keep that clock alive forever, and a session hung at startup
doing exactly this.  The timer now disarms itself the first time it
finds the buffer unseen, and
@code{window-configuration-change-hook} starts it again when the
buffer comes back on screen.

The honest cost: particles advance on wall-clock time inside the render
pass, so the view keeps a redraw clock up at
@code{cmacs-secondbrain-particle-fps} (24) even when nothing is
happening.  Turning particles off stops the clock with them.

The DEFUNs behind them (@code{cmacs-libregnum-particles-enable},
@code{-clear}, @code{-emitter}, @code{-burst}, @code{-count}) are
generic, so any libregnum scene can use them.  Colours are
@code{0xRRGGBBAA}; particles fade to transparent from whatever they
start at.

@subsection The framebuffer must match its window

@code{cmacs-secondbrain--fit-window-now} resizes the framebuffer to the
window's @strong{body} pixel size --- the text area, with fringes,
margins and the mode line excluded --- and
@code{window-size-change-functions} re-runs it, coalesced through a
0.06@tie{}s idle timer, whenever the window changes.

This is not cosmetic, and neither is the word @emph{body}.  The
picture, the click mapping and the framebuffer are a three-party
contract: the pgtk overlay and the @option{--lrg} backend paint the
view into @code{window_box TEXT_AREA}, the input layer maps clicks
against that same box, and this hook sizes the FBO to match.  Any
party on a different rect puts every click somewhere other than where
it was aimed.  Both failure modes have shipped: before the hook
existed nothing was clickable at all, and later the @emph{paint} used
the full window rect against a text-area click mapping --- offset by
the fringes and the mode line's share of the height --- so every node
had to be clicked above and to the right of where it was drawn.

@node Secondbrain Galaxy
@section The 3D view is a galaxy

Concentric rings viewed in three dimensions are coplanar, so orbiting
them only proves they are flat --- the third dimension buys nothing.
So in 3D the disc is @emph{warped}, the way a galaxy is warped.

Two terms, and the split is the design:

@table @asis
@item The warp
A mode-1 bend --- the ``integral sign'' warp real galaxies have.
Height grows with radius and varies with the azimuth, so one side of
the map lifts and the opposite side drops.  Coherent rather than
random: neighbours stay neighbours, a department stays a clump, and the
eye reads a shape instead of scatter.

@item The thickness
A small per-node offset so one department's members are not perfectly
coplanar.  Without it the map is a bent sheet; with it the disc has
substance.  Deliberately well under a row gap --- crank it and the band
structure the layout works hard to produce dissolves back into the
smear it exists to prevent.
@end table

@code{cmacs-secondbrain-galaxy-tilt} (24 degrees) is the warp's crest:
there a node sits exactly that far above the plane.  The thickness
rides on top, so an individual node can sit a few degrees beyond it ---
the number describes the shape of the disc, not a ceiling on any one
node.  @kbd{T} cycles flat, gentle, default, steep.

The height is computed from the node's @emph{final} azimuth, spin
included, so the warp is fixed in world space and a spinning ring flows
through it the way stars orbit through a real warped disc, rather than
the whole sheet rotating rigidly.

Two scope notes.  It applies to the @code{rings} layout only --- a hex
lattice bent along an azimuth it does not have would just be crooked.
And it needs no branch on the view: a 2D layout snaps every node to
@math{z = 0}, so the setting is inert in the flat view and @kbd{v} can
toggle back and forth with nothing to restore.

The per-node thickness is hashed from the node's @emph{id}, not its
index: index order churns on every rebuild, so hashing that would
reshuffle every height on refresh and the map would twitch for no
reason.

@node Secondbrain Layouts
@section Layouts

Four, switchable at any time, and every switch @strong{animates} rather
than teleporting --- a change that moves every node at once gives the eye
no way to follow which node went where.

@table @strong
@item Rings
The ARMS layout, and the default.  Four concentric bands, departments as
contiguous arcs.
@item Circle
One concentric circle per department, ignoring rings.
@item Hex
A hex lattice.  Dense and regular; good for seeing how @emph{many} of
something there is.
@item Force
The same Fruchterman--Reingold solver roamgraph uses, for when you want
the structure to speak rather than the taxonomy.
@end table

Ring spin (@kbd{s} / @kbd{S}) rotates the bands.  Alternate bands
counter-rotate, so the motion reads as depth rather than as the whole
figure sliding.

@node Secondbrain Search
@section Search

Search never re-lays-out the map: it recolours in place, so the shape
you were reading stays the shape you are reading.

@strong{A match inside a collapsed department lights the department.}
Without that the feature is dead on the map people actually see: it
opens fully collapsed, so every hit resolves to a node with no drawable
and the search highlights an empty screen.  Expanding everything
instead is not the answer either --- two thousand hits would bury the
answer in the noise the search exists to cut through.  So the
departments holding your matches light up, and @kbd{n} walks into them
one at a time, expanding as it goes.

Three tiers, cheapest first.  The ordering is the point: most of a
lookup needs no intelligence at all, so spend none.

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Substring} (@kbd{/}) --- instant, over the loaded node set.  Most
of what you look for you already know the name of.
@item
@strong{Semantic} (@kbd{?}) --- embeds the query once and ranks against
the brigade notes index.  Reach for it when you do not know the name.
@item
@strong{Similarity edges}
(@kbd{M-x cmacs-secondbrain-add-similarity-edges}) --- materialises
``these are about the same thing'' as edges you can see.
@end enumerate

Matching nodes take an accent colour and everything else dims, in place
--- recolouring on every keystroke must not mean rebuilding the scene.

@kbd{M-x cmacs-roamgraph-similarity-edges} does the same for the
knowledge graph.  roamgraph has carried the @code{sim} edge kind since
before anything emitted one: parsed, weighted 0.35 in the solver so it
nudges without competing with real structure, rendered violet so it is
never mistaken for a link you wrote, and excluded from @kbd{[} / @kbd{]}
navigation.

@node Secondbrain Focusing
@section Focusing the view

Three ways to make the map answer one question at a time, all composable
with search and with each other:

@table @asis
@item Isolate (@kbd{x})
While a node is selected, only it, its subtree and its direct
neighbours keep their colour; the rest recedes to a third and its links
go near-invisible.  Selecting a @emph{department} isolates the
department and everything it reaches --- ``show me what this connects
to'' as one keystroke.  The mode follows the selection as it moves, and
with nothing selected it is armed but inert.

@item Ring filter (@kbd{F})
Cycles all @arrow{} Applications @arrow{} Routines @arrow{} Memory
@arrow{} Skills @arrow{} all.  Everything outside the kept ring dims.
The centre stays lit: it belongs to every ring, and a filtered map with
its own centre dimmed looks broken rather than filtered.

@item Age fade (@kbd{a})
Recolours Memory by modification time: a note touched today keeps its
full PARA colour, one untouched for
@code{cmacs-secondbrain-age-fade-days} (180) fades most of the way to a
cold grey --- toward grey, not black, because against a dark background
black is @emph{gone} and stale should read as @emph{faded}.  Department
hubs wear their members' @strong{mean} age, which matters because the
default map is fully collapsed: a fade that only touched leaves would
render the default view pixel-identical with the feature on and off.
@end table

Two design rules keep these honest.  First, isolate and the ring filter
are @emph{paint-time decisions}, not flag writes: the DIM flag belongs
to the search machinery, and a second writer would have to agree with it
about when to clear --- the classic two-owners bug.  Second, a search
MATCH lights up through both --- you searched for it, so a filter never
hides it.

The mode line shows @code{[ring]}, @code{[isolate]} and @code{[age]}
while any of them is active.

@node Secondbrain AI
@section AI actions

Right-click any node and the shared AI menu is there, plus four
questions this view makes askable:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Explain this skill / routine / application} --- what it is and
what it is for.
@item
@strong{What would break without this?} --- the trust question the
Applications ring exists for.  An honest ``probably nothing'' is the most
useful answer it can give, because that is what you were looking for.
@item
@strong{Is this stale?} --- superseded, abandoned, or referring to things
that no longer exist.
@item
@strong{Summarise this department} --- offered only on a hub.
@end itemize

All of them answer through @code{cmacs-ai-textops-stream}, so the reply
lands in the output window with @kbd{C-c C-c} to follow up and @kbd{g} to
retry.  Because the view registers a target resolver,
@code{cmacs-ai-view-context} sees it too: a chat turn knows which node
you are looking at without being told.

@node Secondbrain Panes
@section Panes and icons

@kbd{i} opens the inspector on the right: name, role, ring, department,
path, the actions available, and the node's connections in both
directions.  It refreshes as you select --- an inspector showing the
previous node is worse than no inspector, because it is confidently
wrong about what you just clicked.

@kbd{p} opens a preview on the left, rendering the node's file inline
with its own major mode's fontification.  This is what makes a skill
readable without leaving the map.  @kbd{W} closes both.

Other packages add inspector actions with
@code{cmacs-secondbrain-register-inspector-action}, which also installs
the binding for Evil's normal and motion states --- without that half a
registered @kbd{a} would just be @code{evil-append} under Doom.

Applications-ring nodes draw their real SVG logo when one is available,
rasterised through libregnum's @code{LrgVectorImage} and uploaded as a
texture, so it stays crisp at any zoom.  @strong{No icons ship with
cmacs} --- application logos are other people's trademarks.
@code{cmacs-secondbrain-icon-dirs} lists where to look, names are matched
case- and separator-insensitively, and a node without an icon simply
keeps its glyph.

@node Secondbrain Sources
@section Where the data comes from

Everything is a registered @dfn{source}.  Adding a ring member is a
registration, not a patch:

@example
(cmacs-secondbrain-register-source
 :name 'my-thing :ring 'applications :label "My thing"
 :enumerate (lambda () (list (list :id "x" :title "X" :kind 'app))))
@end example

Shipped sources cover two worlds, because both are real: the Claude Code
workspace under @file{~/.claude} (MCP servers, plugins, scheduled tasks,
skills, agents) and cmacs's own equivalents (connected subsystems,
brigade schedules, podomation pods, brigade tools).  The Memory ring
comes from the PARA notes tree.

@code{cmacs-secondbrain-enabled-sources} selects which contribute.  A
source you do not use is not merely noise: an Applications ring listing
things you never connected tells you nothing about your actual trust
surface.

@strong{A source that signals is caught, reported and skipped.}  One
unreadable config file costs you one ring member, not the map.

@subsection Memory is PARA

The Memory ring groups by @ref{CMacs PARA} bucket, with
@file{02_areas/dailies} split out because it is roughly 40% of a mature
tree and would otherwise swamp the areas colour.  The tree is already
organised; grouping it by anything else would be inventing a second
organisation on top of the real one.

Notes are read from @file{org-roam.db} when it exists.  When it does not
--- org-roam builds it on demand, and roamgraph's native C scanner is a
stub --- a built-in scanner walks the tree for @code{:ID:} drawers and
@code{[[id:]]} links instead.  Without that fallback the largest of the
four rings comes up empty on a machine whose notes are perfectly well
formed.

@node Secondbrain Configuration
@section Configuration

Everything below is a @code{defcustom} in the @code{cmacs-secondbrain}
group, so @kbd{M-x customize-group @key{RET} cmacs-secondbrain} shows
the lot with its documentation.  Nothing here needs to be set: the
defaults are what @kbd{M-x cmacs-secondbrain} gives you.

@subsection A worked init.el

@lisp
;; The view opens folded, to the rings and their department hubs.
;; Set this to nil to open the whole graph -- every file and every
;; link -- on load instead.
(setq cmacs-secondbrain-start-collapsed t)

;; Background.  `nebula' is the default: value noise and a star field
;; over a vertical gradient, generated once into a texture and cached.
(setq cmacs-secondbrain-background 'nebula
      cmacs-secondbrain-background-colors '(#x2A3A6BFF . #x05050AFF))

;; Or your own wallpaper, drawn cover-fit so any aspect ratio fills the
;; viewport without being squashed:
;; (setq cmacs-secondbrain-background 'image
;;       cmacs-secondbrain-background-image "~/Pictures/space.png")

;; Or a live screensaver from `cmacs-screensaver-configs'.  Off by
;; default deliberately -- see below.
;; (setq cmacs-secondbrain-background 'screensaver
;;       cmacs-secondbrain-screensaver 'helios-blue)

;; Motion.
(setq cmacs-secondbrain-particles t
      cmacs-secondbrain-link-pulse t
      cmacs-secondbrain-link-pulse-speed 2.4
      cmacs-secondbrain-auto-rotate 0.0     ; degrees/second; `R' toggles
      cmacs-secondbrain-rotate-speed 3.0)

;; Labels scale with the viewport: this is the size at a viewport
;; `-label-reference-height' tall, and it grows from there.
(setq cmacs-secondbrain-label-size 15
      cmacs-secondbrain-max-labels 160)

;; Interaction.
(setq cmacs-secondbrain-node-glow t        ; additive halos (G toggles)
      cmacs-secondbrain-age-fade nil       ; colour Memory by staleness (a)
      cmacs-secondbrain-age-fade-days 180.0
      cmacs-secondbrain-node-shading t
      cmacs-secondbrain-drag-nodes t
      cmacs-secondbrain-hover-highlights-group t
      cmacs-secondbrain-fly-context 0.4)

;; Which rings get populated.  `t' means every registered source; a
;; list names the ones you want.
(setq cmacs-secondbrain-enabled-sources t)
@end lisp

@subsection What costs something

Three settings carry a standing cost rather than a one-off one, which is
why each is off or conservative by default.

@table @code
@item cmacs-secondbrain-background
Set to @code{screensaver} it runs a second process rendering
continuously and holds a redraw clock up for as long as the buffer
lives.  The procedural backgrounds are generated once into a texture and
then cost one blit per frame.
@item cmacs-secondbrain-particles
Holds a redraw clock at @code{-particle-fps} even when nothing is
happening.  That is the honest price of ambient motion; turning
particles off stops the clock.
@item cmacs-secondbrain-auto-rotate
Costs a layout pass per tick, because it turns the layout rather than
the camera --- which is what makes picking follow the nodes.
@end table

@code{cmacs-secondbrain-link-pulse} costs a recolour pass over the edges
per frame, but only while something is selected.

@subsection Turning the whole thing down

For a still, cheap view:

@lisp
(setq cmacs-secondbrain-background 'solid
      cmacs-secondbrain-particles nil
      cmacs-secondbrain-link-pulse nil
      cmacs-secondbrain-auto-rotate 0.0)
@end lisp

With all four off the view redraws only when something changes.

@node Secondbrain Reading
@section Reading the map

Shape is the primary signal; the map should be readable before any label
resolves.  A hex prism is an application, a sphere with a wide ring a
collapsed department, a plain sphere a note or file, a faceted gem a
skill.

A collapsed hub's radius grows with the @strong{cube root} of its
descendant count: a department hiding 12000 files must read as bigger
than one hiding 300 without swallowing the frame.  Linear makes the large
one absurd; logarithmic flattens the two into the same dot.

Colours are per ring, fixed rather than hashed, so a colour means the
same thing in every session.  Memory nodes take their PARA bucket's
colour instead.

@node Secondbrain MCP
@section MCP tools

With @option{--with-cmacs-mcp}: @code{secondbrain_open},
@code{secondbrain_set_layout}, @code{secondbrain_search},
@code{secondbrain_expand}, @code{secondbrain_node_info},
@code{secondbrain_stats}, @code{secondbrain_sources} and
@code{secondbrain_refresh}.

Every interpolated argument goes through the Elisp string escaper: these
are attacker-controlled tool arguments being spliced into a form that is
about to be evaluated.

@node Secondbrain Extending
@section Extending

A data source is the recipe above --- no C.  An AI action registers
through @code{cmacs-ai-register-action} so it appears everywhere the
shared menu is built.  A new glyph does need C: add to
@code{CmacsSbKind} and its name table in
@file{cmacs-secondbrain-model.c}, add a case to @code{glyph_for}, and
give it a base radius.

libregnum's shape set is spheres, icospheres, cubes, cylinders (a
6-slice cylinder is a hex prism), cones, planes, grids, lines and
circles.  There is no torus.

What you cannot extend from Elisp: glyph geometry, layout kinds (those
live in graphcore), edge routing, and the pick ray.

@node Secondbrain Architecture
@section Architecture

Three translation-unit classes, the roamgraph split:

@table @file
@item cmacs-secondbrain-scene.c
Includes @file{libregnum.h}, so it may not include @file{lisp.h} ---
raylib's @code{Color} clashes with pgtk's.
@item cmacs-secondbrain-defuns.c
Includes @file{lisp.h} only; the Elisp boundary.
@item cmacs-secondbrain-model.c
Includes @emph{neither}, only glib: the ARMS ring vocabulary, testable
headless.
@end table

C owns geometry, the rings, the pick ray and the node table.  Elisp owns
where the data comes from, what a click means and every pane.  C knows
nothing about MCP servers, cron or org files.  The contract is one DEFUN,
@code{cmacs-secondbrain-set-graph}.

@subsection Identity

libregnum scene node ids are insertion indices and churn on @emph{every}
rebuild, so every piece of state keys on the source-assigned id string
--- which is what the C side stores as the node's @code{path}, and what
arrives synchronously in the click dispatch.

@subsection Emission order is not graph order

A collapsed department hides its whole subtree, so the scene's node table
is in emission order while the graph is in graph order.  The scene keeps
explicit @code{node_emit} / @code{edge_emit} maps; reading node flags with
a graph index would recolour the wrong nodes.

@node Secondbrain Not Implemented
@section Not implemented

@itemize @bullet
@item
Skills are faceted gems, not four-pointed stars --- that needs a custom
mesh.
@item
No icons ship with cmacs --- application logos are other people's
trademarks.  @code{cmacs-secondbrain-icon-dirs} points at where you may
already have them; a node without one keeps its glyph.
@item
No node dragging --- @code{dragging_object} is gated on
@code{editor_active} in the libregnum input layer.
@item
No folders view; departments group by PARA bucket or by source, not by
the literal directory tree.
@item
Similarity edges are computed on demand, not maintained as the notes
change.
@end itemize

@section Tests

@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-secondbrain-tests.el}, 24 tests in three tiers:
the source registry and collection (pure data, always runs), the scanner
(a temporary tree), and the viewport (needs a display).  Plus
@command{just test-graphcore} for the shared engine --- 25 more, fully
headless.
